  "raw_text": "Step 1: Convert the binary number \\(10101001110_2\\) into its decimal (base 10) equivalent first.\n\nStep 2: To convert from binary to decimal, we use the formula where each digit is multiplied by \\(2^n\\), with \\(n\\) being the position of the digit, starting from 0 on the right. So, for \\(10101001110_2\\):\n\n\\[\n1 \\cdot 2^{10} + 0 \\cdot 2^9 + 1 \\cdot 2^8 + 0 \\cdot 2^7 + 1 \\cdot 2^6 + 0 \\cdot 2^5 + 0 \\cdot 2^4 + 1 \\cdot 2^3 + 1 \\cdot 2^2 + 1 \\cdot 2^1 + 0 \\cdot 2^0\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Calculate each term:\n\n\\[\n1 \\cdot 1024 + 0 \\cdot 512 + 1 \\cdot 256 + 0 \\cdot 128 + 1 \\cdot 64 + 0 \\cdot 32 + 0 \\cdot 16 + 1 \\cdot 8 + 1 \\cdot 4 + 1 \\cdot 2 + 0 \\cdot 1\n\\]\n\nStep 4: Sum these values:\n\n\\[\n1024 + 0 + 256 + 0 + 64 + 0 + 0 + 8 + 4 + 2 + 0 = 1358\n\\]\n\nStep 5: Now that we have the decimal number 1358, we need to convert it to base 8. We do this by repeatedly dividing the number by 8 and recording the remainders.\n\nStep 6: Divide 1358 by 8:\n\n\\[\n1358 \\div 8 = 169 \\text{ remainder } 6\n\\]\n\nStep 7: Divide 169 by 8:\n\n\\[\n169 \\div 8 = 21 \\text{ remainder } 1\n\\]\n\nStep 8: Divide 21 by 8:\n\n\\[\n21 \\div 8 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 5\n\\]\n\nStep 9: Divide 2 by 8:\n\n\\[\n2 \\div 8 = 0 \\text{ remainder } 2\n\\]\n\nStep 10: Reading the remainders from bottom to top gives us the base 8 representation: \\(2516_8\\).\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{2516}<|im_end|>",
